WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.169
As anyone familiar with series creator George
Lucas' original Star Wars screenplay would

00:00:06.169 --> 00:00:10.940
tell you, the process of making the first
film so special involved throwing a lot of

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:12.520
bad ideas away.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:16.740
So while most of the series' worst moments
are confined to the three prequels, the movie

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:22.330
that started it all had plenty of cringeworthy
material going into it, which many of Lucas'

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:25.339
collaborators wisely fought to prune.

00:00:25.339 --> 00:00:30.090
Appearing on The Tonight Show Starring Johnny
Carson shortly after Star Wars mania had begun

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:36.370
to sweep the nation in 1977, Mark Hamill shared
his own contribution to the script's improvement,

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:41.330
revealing one clunker of a line from the original
screenplay he talked Lucas out of.

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:43.200
"The dialogue was a little bit difficult.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.520
I remember there was one line that I just
begged [Lucas] to take out of the screenplay—and

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:48.530
he finally did."

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:50.600
"I'll never forget it as long as I live.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.960
I sometimes dream about this line."

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:57.280
The passage in question came up during a scene
in which the crew of the Millennium Falcon

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.680
comes across what remains of Princess Leia's
home planet, Alderaan, after it's been destroyed

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:03.860
by the Death Star.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:09.280
The line Hamill protested came as a response
from Luke to a remark by Han Solo, which Hamill

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.979
quotes for Carson with a charmingly gruff
Harrison Ford impression.

00:01:12.979 --> 00:01:16.740
"Harrison says, 'Look kid, I've done my part
of the bargain.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.950
When I get to an asteroid, you, the old man,
and the droids get dropped off."

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:26.049
That line in and of itself wouldn't have been
too out of the ordinary for the swashbuckling

00:01:26.049 --> 00:01:27.049
spaceman.

00:01:27.049 --> 00:01:29.590
But what would've come next was pretty ridiculous.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:35.189
"And my line was: 'But we can't turn back,
fear is their greatest defense; I doubt if

00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:38.329
the actual security there is any greater than
it was on Aquilae or Sullust, and what there

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:40.990
is is most likely directed towards a large-scale
assault!'"

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:45.990
Even in a script filled with confusing exchanges,
that one was too much for Hamill.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:50.340
"And I thought, 'Who talks like this, George?'"

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:54.150
The line would've still been far from the
worst bit of dialogue ever to be uttered in

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:59.009
the series—but you can see why Hamill wouldn't
want to try and deliver those words.

00:01:59.009 --> 00:02:02.380
Hamill remembered pleading for the line's
removal out of a sense of self-preservation.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:04.539
"This is really not fair.

00:02:04.539 --> 00:02:10.039
We're the ones who are going to get vegetables
thrown at us—not you."

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:14.650
The presence of the planet names "Aquilae"
and "Sullust" in the convoluted line serves

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:19.150
to demonstrate just how much got chiseled
away from Star Wars' script on way from page

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:20.290
to screen.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:25.480
In the original story treatment, Aquilae was
a fleshed-out planet with the real-world parallel

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.040
of communist North Vietnam.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:31.909
By the time the movie was shooting, the names
had been reduced to a reference for the sake

00:02:31.909 --> 00:02:32.909
of worldbuilding.

00:02:32.909 --> 00:02:37.150
When Hamill prevailed in removing the one
line they appeared in, he deleted the planets

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:38.549
entirely.

00:02:38.549 --> 00:02:43.269
The line that haunted Hamill isn't the only
part of Star Wars' original script that needed

00:02:43.269 --> 00:02:46.120
work, and Hamill's not the only actor who
had issues.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:50.031
"Harrison Ford, who plays the space pirate
in the film, at one point threatened to tie

00:02:50.031 --> 00:02:53.879
George up and make him say his own lines at
gunpoint."

00:02:53.879 --> 00:02:58.520
While the cast's complaints with the script
may have caused some friction, their efforts

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:03.769
worked to sand down Star Wars' rough edges,
making it so most of the worst stuff never

00:03:03.769 --> 00:03:05.659
made it out of the editing bay.

00:03:05.659 --> 00:03:10.019
It all goes to show that when it comes to
collaboration, compromise is king.

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:15.349
Otherwise you get The Phantom Menace—and
how many great lines can you quote from that?

00:03:15.349 --> 00:03:16.980
"Issa big sea monster!

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:18.960
Jar Jar Binks is terrible."

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:20.360
Thanks for watching!

